<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakht Buland Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1709</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bakht Buland Shah was a Gond king of Deogarh in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries. He served in the army of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim settlers to clear and farm land. Around 1702 he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, which later became the capital of the Bhonsle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakht-buland-shah.jpg" alt="Bakht Buland Shah" width="319" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S. Deogaonkar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portait_of_Bakht_Buland_Shah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
